digraph ReasoningTree {
  // paper_id=s41467-025-56667-3
  rankdir="TB";
  "Abduc1" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction (0,0,0): Observation: Nano/micro-structures must be preserved before main pulse arrival. Known principle: High temporal contrast suppresses prepulse below damage threshold. Therefore, high temporal contrast is required to preserve structures."];
  "Abduc3" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction (0,0,0): Observation: Energetic protons achieved from nanometer-thick structured foils. Known principle: Suppressing cold electron conduction enhances Coulomb repulsion in hybrid acceleration. Therefore, conduction-restricted nanometer targets enhance proton energy via hybrid mechanism."];
  "Case1" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-case (9,0,0): Different coating structures including surface grating, carbon nanotube foam, and arrays of microwire or microtube have been attempted to modify target front surface"];
  "Case2" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-case (12,0,0): Microspheres and cryogenic hydrogen jets are used as isolated high-repetition targets where protons are accelerated via collisionless shock acceleration or Coulomb explosion"];
  "Case4" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-case (14,0,0): Several experiments tried femtosecond driving laser with power <200 TW"];
  "Case5" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction-case (18,0,0): Currently, the peak intensity of multi-petawatt laser is orders of magnitude higher than terawatt pulses"];
  "Common1" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-common (8,0,0): Several effective designs of structured targets have been demonstrated for increasing proton energy"];
  "Common2" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-common (11,0,0): A number of experiments have employed mass-limited targets to improve proton quality"];
  "Common3" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-common (14,0,0): The use of femtosecond driving laser with power <200 TW demonstrates robust enhancement of proton energy"];
  "Deduc3" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction (0,0,0): Currently, multi-petawatt lasers have orders of magnitude higher peak intensity than terawatt pulses. If peak intensity is much higher, then acceleration mechanisms may be novel and complex. Therefore, understanding the acceleration process is critical for optimization."];
  "Fact1" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction-case (5,0,0): Currently, limitations including lack of long acceleration structure, deflection by self-generated magnetic field, and dephasing lead to proton energy below 100 MeV in experiments (except one 150-MeV case)"];
  "Know1" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction-knowledge (16,0,0): High temporal contrast of laser is prerequisite because prepulse intensity must be suppressed below the damage threshold of structured targets"];
  "Know2" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction-knowledge (17,0,0): Maintaining excellent laser pointing accuracy within a few microns is necessary for precise alignment"];
  "Know3" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction-knowledge (22,0,0): Coulomb repulsion is enhanced in laser-tailored foils by suppressing the conduction of cold electrons, contributing to hybrid acceleration mechanism"];
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) The paper investigates and evaluates ultrathin, laser-tailored polymer-foil targets irradiated by a high-contrast multi-petawatt femtosecond laser as a practical route to >100 MeV laser-driven proton beams, motivated by restricted cold-electron conduction and a hybrid radiation-pressure–Coulomb-repulsion proton-acceleration process.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) When higher laser-driven proton energies are needed, target tailoring, high-contrast multi-petawatt femtosecond irradiation, precise alignment, and control of electron-conduction and Coulomb-repulsion effects jointly motivate investigating hybrid acceleration mechanisms in ultrathin foils."];
  "Need1" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction (0,0,0): Currently, proton energies are limited to <100 MeV in most experiments, and applications like radiography of dense objects and therapy of deep-seated tumors require higher energies. If improving proton energy is essential for these applications, then research must focus on overcoming current limitations. Therefore, research must focus on overcoming current limitations to improve proton energy."];
  "Phenom1" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction-phenomenon (15,0,0): The major challenge is preserving nano- or micro-structures before the main pulse arrival when using petawatt femtosecond laser on structured targets"];
  "Phenom2" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction-phenomenon (17,0,0): Precise alignment of focal spot on target structures is crucial"];
  "Phenom3" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction-phenomenon (21,0,0): 110-MeV protons were successfully measured from 100-nm-thick, 30-μm-wide foil"];
  "R11" [label="(0,0,0) The measurement of 110-MeV protons from a 100-nm-thick, 30-μm-wide foil is consistent with the interpretation that suppressing cold-electron conduction in laser-tailored foils enhances Coulomb repulsion within a hybrid acceleration mechanism."];
  "R2" [label="(0,0,0) Structured target designs, including front-surface structures such as surface gratings, carbon nanotube foam, microwire arrays, and microtubes, have been explored as target modifications, and several structured-target designs have been reported effective for increasing proton energy."];
  "R3" [label="(0,0,0) Mass-limited or isolated targets such as microspheres and cryogenic hydrogen jets are an explored experimental approach for improving proton-beam quality, including regimes where protons are accelerated by collisionless shock acceleration or Coulomb explosion."];
  "R5" [label="(0,0,0) Experiments using femtosecond driving lasers with power below 200 TW provide a constrained basis for considering femtosecond-laser irradiation as a route to proton-energy enhancement under that power regime."];
  "R7" [label="(0,0,0) Because precise focal-spot alignment on target structures is crucial, the requirement for excellent laser pointing accuracy within a few microns is a key experimental constraint for structured-target irradiation."];
  "Rule1" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction-rule (6,0,0): If improving the energy of laser-accelerated protons is essential for applications, especially for instantaneous radiography of dense objects and clinical therapy of deep-seated tumors, then research must focus on overcoming current limitations"];
  "Rule3" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction-rule (18,0,0): If peak intensity is much higher, then proton acceleration may occur via novel and complex mechanism, and proper understanding is critical for optimization"];
  "Fact1" -> "Need1" [label="deduction-case"];
  "Rule1" -> "Need1" [label="deduction-rule"];
  "Phenom1" -> "Abduc1" [label="abduction-phenomenon"];
  "Know1" -> "Abduc1" [label="abduction-knowledge"];
  "Case5" -> "Deduc3" [label="deduction-case"];
  "Rule3" -> "Deduc3" [label="deduction-rule"];
  "Phenom3" -> "Abduc3" [label="abduction-phenomenon"];
  "Know3" -> "Abduc3" [label="abduction-knowledge"];
  "Case1" -> "R2" [label="induction-case"];
  "Common1" -> "R2" [label="induction-common"];
  "Case2" -> "R3" [label="induction-case"];
  "Common2" -> "R3" [label="induction-common"];
